    "abstract": "<jats:title>Abstract</jats:title>\n                  <jats:p>\n                    Perennial cellulosic bioenergy crops have the potential to control nutrient losses from agriculture while providing biomass for biofuel production. This study employed a rigorously calibrated Soil and Water Assessment Tool (SWAT) model to assess the effects of replacing conventional corn (\n                    <jats:italic>Zea mays</jats:italic>\n                    L.) with\n                    <jats:italic>Miscanthus</jats:italic>\n                    \u00a0\u00d7\u00a0\n                    <jats:italic>giganteus</jats:italic>\n                    and Alamo switchgrass (\n                    <jats:italic>Panicum virgatum</jats:italic>\n                    L.) on nitrogen (N) and phosphorus (P) losses across three slope categories (&lt;2%, 2%\u20134%, and &gt;4%) within the Upper Mississippi River Basin (UMRB), a typical Mollisol region with intensive agricultural production. Model results indicated that N and P losses were effectively reduced by replacing corn with two bioenergy crops in 1984\u20132018. Organic P loss, dissolved inorganic P loss, and nitrate N (NO\n                    <jats:sub>3</jats:sub>\n                    \u2010N) loss by runoff were reduced by an average of over 93%, with more significant reductions occurring at higher slopes. NO\n                    <jats:sub>3</jats:sub>\n                    \u2010N leaching was reduced in most zones. Notably, replacing corn with\n                    <jats:italic>Miscanthus</jats:italic>\n                    and switchgrass achieved economic benefits from biofuel production of 3,000\u20136,000 US$/ha and environmental benefits from savings in pollution\u2010control costs of approximately 2,700 US$/ha. By a trade\u2010off analysis between environmental and economic benefits,\n                    <jats:italic>Miscanthus</jats:italic>\n                    was suitably planted in the western and downstream zones of the basin with single and double harvesting, and switchgrass was suitably planted in the northeastern zones with single harvesting, achieving a total attainable economic benefit of 24.2 billion US$. These findings emphasize that replacing corn with perennial bioenergy crops on sloping farmland with high risk of nutrient loss and low productivity can provide a win\u2010win situation for environmental protection and agricultural sustainability.\n                  </jats:p>",
